Task 8: pin the read contract in the packages that own it

The end-to-end tests prove a read works; they cannot say which part of the
answer a version that broke it dropped. The answer is a frame sequence, so
a part left unsent does not stay put -- it becomes the next answer, and the
symptom appears somewhere else entirely.

internal/server/read_test.go drives a raw connection as the owner and as the
requester, and asserts all three parts, the ID and kind on every path: a
successful read, an empty file, a failed read, an unknown client, a rejected
request, an empty file followed by a full one, and three reads in a row on
one connection. Skipping the owner's closing frame fails the in-order test.

internal/viewer/read_test.go drives the viewer against a fake TCP server, so
the frame sequence is exercised without a second implementation in the way.
It covers the successful read, the empty file, a head-frame failure, two
reads on one connection, and that a range is both honoured and encoded.

cmd/peerfold/cmd_cat_test.go covers the command: the bytes reach stdout
unchanged (including NUL and invalid UTF-8), an empty file is a success with
empty output, and missing paths, unknown clients, unreachable servers, a
missing configured server and bad argument counts are all failures that say
something. Treating an empty stream as an error fails the empty-file test.

Verified: gofmt clean, go vet 0, go test ./... green.